took my hand and held it, hard. Her mouth was doing something it
hadn’t done since my father’s funeral.
   “Sabía,” she said. Barely a breath. “I knew it was something with a
name.”
   She didn’t say anything else. She didn’t need to. She got up, and she
put more rice on my plate, and when I said I was full she gave me a
look, so I ate it.
   The phone rang halfway through the flan.
   It was her phone, face-up by the napkin holder, and the screen said
MAMI. My mother looked at it for exactly one ring. Then she picked it
up, didn’t answer it, and held it out to me across the table.
   “She wants to talk to you.”
   I took it. My hands didn’t feel like mine.
   “Abuela?”
   She didn’t say hello. Her voice, thin and steady, with a radio going
somewhere behind it and the faint sound of a river, which I may have
invented, said:
   “Ya empezó.”
   She said it like a fact she was reading off a page.
   “Sí, Abuela.”
   A breath. A long one. I could hear a woman in a kitchen on an is‐
land putting down a thing she’d carried for a very long time.
   “Ven,” she said. “Pronto.”
   “I have three gigs. I have a—”
   “Los gigs se quedan,” said my grandmother. “Yo no.”
   “What is the coin, Abuela? What are the señales?”
   “Es un pedazo de la Madre. Ya despertó. Lo demás te lo cuento
aquí.”
   The line clicked. I sat there with the phone against my ear and the
flan getting warm. My mother crossed herself without looking at me.
Then she cleared the plates very loudly, not looking at me, in the way
of a woman who has heard every word of a conversation she wasn’t on.
   On the stoop below, someone had a radio on. Under it, in the same
place every eight seconds, a thumb on a scale.




